{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778875817,"nonce":"LQpS+YTMwzZ2bDrS5v5+/m1rjHY=","title":"Umbrella: Feedbax/Mandible MVP integration roadmap","message":"## Read This First\n\nThis is the cross-repo implementation roadmap for the Feedbax + Feedbax Studio + Mandible MVP. It is meant to be the onboarding issue for future agents: start here, then follow the linked spec/implementation issues for the repo you are working in.\n\nThe goal is not to implement five specs independently. The goal is to build one coherent vertical path, with the minimal durable contracts in place before richer UI work.\n\n## MVP Success Criterion\n\nA first useful MVP means:\n\n1. A Feedbax spec/scenario can be created or loaded.\n2. Feedbax can validate it through a provider/registry contract.\n3. A small local or remote training/evaluation/analysis flow can emit durable manifests and artifact references.\n4. Mandible can ingest those manifests/artifacts and show the correct relationships in its graph workspace.\n5. Feedbax Studio can operate over the same specs/manifests rather than hidden panel-local state.\n6. The user can inspect what happened: issue/session/spec/run/eval/analysis/report/artifact relationships are visible and explainable.\n\nThe first vertical slice can use toy or small CPU-local workloads where necessary. Cloud execution matters, but it should be added through a deterministic worker/provider contract rather than by having agents improvise setup every time.\n\n## Canonical Specs and Issues\n\nFeedbax-owned specs/issues:\n\n- `c6c6da0` — spec: Feedbax manifests and provider contract. Foundation for manifests, artifacts, provider capabilities, registries, and standalone operation without Mandible.\n- `0c46f69` — spec: Feedbax Studio pipeline workspace. Bottom pane as stage/collection/provenance workspace.\n- `5378a5d` — spec: Feedbax Studio structural authoring workspace. Top pane as stage-aware scenario editor with task/biomechanics/objective/probe/time layers.\n- `f68cf66` — expose Feedbax as a Mandible plugin/provider for experiment specs, components, runs, and artifacts.\n- `93f79f2` — Feedbax Studio design coordination.\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax design coordination.\n\nMandible-owned specs/issues:\n\n- Mandible `de2f8ac` — spec: Mandible graph workspace and provider/artifact substrate.\n- Mandible `2472c51` — Mandible plugin ecosystem and artifact store for research tool providers.\n- Mandible `0516fc0` — Mandible design coordination.\n\nRelated existing Feedbax implementation context:\n\n- Existing Studio remote/cloud worker issues are mostly closed historical work, not the final deterministic remote execution contract: `16e7203`, `7e013c9`, `20a4b8c`, `8e06c06`.\n- Existing graph-as-model/worker issues remain relevant where they affect lowering real graph specs into training execution, especially `4178e12` and related closed graph-as-model fixes.\n\n## Implementation Order\n\n### Phase 0 — Orientation and Split\n\nRepo: Feedbax first, then Mandible when assigned Mandible work.\n\nBefore implementation, the agent should:\n\n- Read this issue.\n- Read `c6c6da0`, `0c46f69`, `5378a5d`, and the relevant Mandible spec if the task crosses into Mandible.\n- Inspect current code paths before editing; do not rely only on the spec text.\n- Create/split concrete implementation issues for the assigned phase if they do not already exist.\n- Keep repo ownership clear: Feedbax implements domain specs/execution/Studio; Mandible implements provider registry, artifact store, graph ingestion/view, and reviewed provider actions.\n\nDone means: the agent can state which phase it is implementing, which repo owns it, which issue tracks it, and what the smallest testable slice is.\n\n### Phase 1 — Feedbax Manifest/Provider MVP\n\nRepo: Feedbax.\n\nPrimary specs/issues: `c6c6da0`, `f68cf66`, `8d94424`.\n\nBuild the minimal provider substrate before major Studio refactors:\n\n- Manifest models for specs/runs/evals/analyses/reports, or at least the first subset needed for a vertical slice.\n- `ArtifactRef` and shared provenance fields.\n- Provider capability manifest: identity, health, schemas, validation, supported artifact roles, and entry points.\n- Registry snapshots for components/tasks/losses/analyses/protocols, initially wrapping current mechanisms where possible.\n- Validation functions/endpoints/CLI for graph/training/task/loss/eval/analysis specs.\n- Compatibility adapters for current `GraphSpec`, `TrainingSpec`, `TaskSpec`, `LossTermSpec`, analysis modules, and database records.\n\nDo not start by redesigning the whole Studio UI. First prove that Feedbax can describe, validate, execute, and emit durable records through a contract.\n\nDone means: Feedbax can emit a provider manifest, validate a small training/eval/analysis spec, and write or return at least one durable manifest with artifact refs and provenance edges.\n\n### Phase 2 — Mandible Graph + Provider/Artifact MVP\n\nRepo: Mandible.\n\nPrimary specs/issues: Mandible `de2f8ac`, Mandible `2472c51`; Feedbax references `c6c6da0` and this roadmap.\n\nThis should come early. Mandible should not ingest provider outputs that users cannot inspect.\n\nImplement the smallest graph/provider substrate that can display Feedbax relationships:\n\n- Typed graph entity/edge payloads beyond issue/session/auth-only nodes.\n- Graph query modes, at least current activity/work plus a provider/provenance-oriented mode.\n- Selected-node detail pane and hover preview sufficient for specs/runs/artifacts.\n- Provider registry/static provider manifest loader.\n- Provider health/capability API.\n- Local content-addressed artifact ingest and artifact records.\n- Provider manifest ingest that creates graph nodes/edges without Mandible knowing Feedbax internals.\n- Action descriptors for compatible provider actions such as open/validate/preview.\n\nKeep the current stack unless there is a measured reason not to: Node/TypeScript daemon, SQLite, Preact, D3/SVG are acceptable for this phase.\n\nDone means: given a small Feedbax provider manifest plus a training/eval/analysis manifest and artifact refs, Mandible can ingest them and show provider/spec/run/artifact relationships in a graph view with a usable detail pane.\n\n### Phase 3 — Remote/Cloud Execution Contract\n\nRepo: Feedbax, with Mandible integration later for reviewed/credentialed actions.\n\nPrimary specs/issues: this roadmap plus `c6c6da0`; file a dedicated Feedbax issue/spec before broad implementation if none exists.\n\nRemote training should not depend on an agent improvising RunPod setup. Implement a deterministic remote execution contract with two backends:\n\n- Generic SSH worker: user supplies an existing host, port, key, and environment constraints; Feedbax verifies/install-starts a worker, checks health, reports capabilities, runs jobs, streams logs, syncs artifacts, emits manifests.\n- Managed provider adapter: RunPod first, because it matches current usage. Later providers can implement the same adapter shape.\n\nThe deterministic part is the worker bootstrap and verification path:\n\n- Pinned image or setup script.\n- `uv`-based environment setup.\n- CUDA/JAX device verification where relevant.\n- Worker health endpoint and version/capability report.\n- Explicit GPU/driver/JAX/platform metadata in manifests.\n- Log streaming and resumable status checks.\n- Artifact sync with hashes.\n- Failure diagnostics that distinguish allocation failure, SSH failure, environment failure, JAX/GPU failure, worker failure, and training failure.\n\nRunPod specifics should be an adapter, not hardcoded throughout Studio. Current rlrmp RunPod practice can inform the adapter, especially 4090/5090 single-GPU workflows, SSH readiness checks, `uv sync`, CUDA JAX verification, and artifact sync discipline.\n\nDone means: a small Feedbax job can run on either a local worker or a supplied SSH worker using the same execution spec shape, and the result emits manifests/artifact refs compatible with Mandible ingest. RunPod can be the first managed adapter, but generic SSH should exist so the system is not locked to one provider.\n\n### Phase 4 — Feedbax Studio Vertical Slice\n\nRepo: Feedbax.\n\nPrimary specs/issues: `0c46f69`, `5378a5d`, `c6c6da0`, plus the concrete implementation issues split from them.\n\nBuild an ugly but real Studio path over the new contracts:\n\n```text\nscenario/spec -\u003e train run set -\u003e eval selected run set -\u003e analysis -\u003e report/product stub\n```\n\nThe vertical slice should prove:\n\n- Top-pane scenario state is typed and saved.\n- Bottom-pane pipeline stages operate on collections, not one global run.\n- Eval stages can inherit a training scenario and fork explicit overrides.\n- Analysis consumes selected eval/artifact collections.\n- Executions emit manifests and artifact refs.\n- Studio can run without Mandible, but Mandible can ingest the outputs.\n\nKeep UI richness modest. Use existing panels/components as temporary editors where useful, but make data ownership correct.\n\nDone means: from Studio, the user can create/load a simple scenario, launch or register a small training/eval/analysis flow, see stage outputs as collections, and produce manifests/artifacts that Mandible can display.\n\n### Phase 5 — Studio Pipeline/Scenario Refactor\n\nRepo: Feedbax.\n\nPrimary specs/issues: `0c46f69`, `5378a5d`, `93f79f2`.\n\nOnce the vertical slice exists, migrate current Studio ownership into the intended architecture:\n\n- `trainingStore.trainingSpec/taskSpec` becomes stage/scenario-owned state.\n- Loss/task authoring moves toward `ScenarioSpec`/`ObjectiveTermSpec`/typed selectors.\n- `runStore` becomes collection/selection state attached to stages.\n- Analysis pages become analysis-stage specs over selected input collections.\n- Autosave persists workspace/stages/scenarios/spec drafts, not only graph and analysis pages.\n- Top pane becomes stage-aware structural/scenario authoring.\n- Bottom pane becomes pipeline/provenance workspace.\n\nDone means: the visible Studio UI and saved project model agree about stage/scenario ownership. There is no separate hidden frontend interpretation path for executable domain state.\n\n### Phase 6 — Richer UX and Workflow Integration\n\nRepos: Feedbax and Mandible.\n\nOnly after the core vertical path is real, enrich the UX:\n\n- Mandible graph filters, clustering, previews, inspectors, provider actions, curated semantic edges.\n- Studio visual objective authoring: draw end-effector-to-goal objectives, flags/overlays, temporal controls.\n- Biomechanics/task workspace editing and richer 2D/3D views.\n- Report authoring and report product management.\n- Better remote execution provisioning UX.\n- Agent-assisted spec generation and reviewed extension registration.\n\nDone means: the MVP workflow becomes efficient and pleasant, but does not introduce new hidden contracts that bypass manifests/provider validation.\n\n## Cross-Repo Boundary Rules\n\nFeedbax owns:\n\n- Feedbax schemas and validation.\n- Component/task/loss/analysis/protocol registries.\n- Model/task/objective/analysis/report semantics.\n- Studio authoring and execution surfaces.\n- Emitted manifests and local artifact refs.\n- Local/remote worker execution contracts.\n\nMandible owns:\n\n- Provider registry and health/capability discovery.\n- Artifact store and stable artifact IDs.\n- Manifest/artifact ingest.\n- Graph/provenance indexing and graph workspace.\n- Issue/session/auth/work graph relationships.\n- Review/auth policy for provider actions.\n- Agent-facing provider/action discovery.\n\nNeither side should hardcode the other's internals. Feedbax should be usable without Mandible. Mandible should support Feedbax as the first provider without making Feedbax a hard dependency.\n\n## What Agents Should Do When Starting\n\nIf assigned this roadmap generally:\n\n1. Start with Phase 1 in Feedbax unless the user explicitly assigns a later phase.\n2. Create or reuse a concrete implementation issue for the phase before coding.\n3. Keep the first slice small enough to verify end to end.\n4. Preserve compatibility with current Studio and backend objects where possible.\n5. Add tests/fixtures around schema validation, manifest emission, ingestion, and round trips.\n6. Do not polish large UI surfaces before the data contracts and vertical path are real.\n7. If the work moves into Mandible, switch to Mandible's repo instructions and track the Mandible-side work on Mandible issues, cross-linking back here.\n\nIf assigned Mandible graph/provider work:\n\n1. Read Mandible `de2f8ac` and `2472c51` first.\n2. Implement graph/provider/artifact substrate slices there.\n3. Cross-link progress back to this roadmap and to Feedbax `c6c6da0` when Feedbax manifests/providers are involved.\n\nIf assigned Studio work:\n\n1. Read `0c46f69` and `5378a5d` first.\n2. Check whether Phase 1 contracts exist; if not, either implement the required slice or explicitly build a temporary adapter that will be removed.\n3. Keep visible UI state and backend specs in parity from the start.\n\n## Open Decisions to Ask MLL About\n\nAsk before making these durable choices:\n\n- Exact first Studio bottom-pane visualization: timeline, graph, table-plus-detail, or hybrid.\n- Whether the Mandible graph workspace should become the default landing view.\n- Which existing Mandible views should remain separate list-first views.\n- How much biomechanics editing belongs in the first Studio refactor.\n- Whether evaluation manifests should batch many models or represent one model per evaluation under a stage collection.\n- Default artifact retention policy.\n- Which provider actions require review/auth versus ordinary confirmation.\n- How opinionated the first RunPod adapter should be about images, GPU classes, and setup scripts.\n\n## Non-Goals for the First MVP\n\n- No full rewrite of Mandible's UI stack.\n- No full rewrite of Feedbax's execution internals unless a current path blocks manifest/provider correctness.\n- No fully polished Studio visual authoring before stage/scenario specs exist.\n- No provider-specific hardcoding in Mandible graph rendering.\n- No RunPod-only execution architecture.\n- No hidden frontend-only state for executable Studio semantics.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}